The attached image presents box plots illustrating anti-recombinant protective antigen (rPA) IgG antibody titers in mice immunized with soluble rPA alone or rPA adsorbed to varying doses of aluminum hydroxide (AH) or Nano-11 nanoparticles. The results show that Nano-11, similar to AH, significantly enhances the antibody response to rPA compared to soluble rPA. Specifically, Nano-11 formulations (200, 50, and 12.5 µg/dose) induced robust anti-rPA IgG titers, with statistical significance (p < 0.01) compared to soluble rPA, demonstrating its efficacy as a vaccine adjuvant.

The attached image significantly contributes to vaccine development by directly demonstrating the ability of Nano-11 nanoparticles to act as an effective adjuvant, promoting robust antigen-specific antibody responses in vivo. This evidence supports Nano-11 as a promising, safe, and cost-effective alternative to traditional adjuvants like aluminum hydroxide, particularly for subunit vaccines. Its efficacy in enhancing humoral immunity against a relevant antigen (rPA) highlights its potential for developing new prophylactic vaccines.